c) The retirement of the disabled and the retirement by sum 85/95 do not require removal from work and, in a way, are equivalent retirements to special, including in the calculation format (without a previdenciário factor). On the other hand, the insured who converts his special time into common and reaches the points of the sum 85/95, would be benefited compared to the insured who does not do so, since one can remain in work and the other not, violating the principles of equality and isonomy;
d) It is in the interest of the previdenciária revenue that the special retired insured remains, if he wishes, working, since he will be contributing to social security already retired;
e) It is in the interest of society that professionals with experience and highly specialized can maintain their jobs, contributing to the good provision of services and the social welfare of the communities where they act;
f) One cannot force a non-invalid and specialized worker to give up food and salary; on the contrary, the right of choice of the insured to remain in the work environment, if he so desires, must be guaranteed, valuing the principle of individual freedom, which in this case does not harm, but rather points in the same direction of the collective interest.
g) The Federal Supreme Court, when deciding on theme 888, in general repercussion, allowed public servants to gain the permanence bonus for special retirees, that is, the continuity in special labor. It would be discriminatory to deny the same to the private initiative worker.
h) The revocation of the provision will bring greater legal security and will reduce the number of judicial actions, since it will be clear, through legal means and no longer judicial, that it is lawful to remain in special labor.
In light of these arguments, we request our noble colleagues to approve this matter.
