The National Congress decrees:

Article 1: This law regulates the practice of the profession of ceremonialist.

Article 2: The activities and duties of the professionals covered by this law consist of:

I – Planning, research, administration, coordination, and execution of ceremonial projects;

II – Preparation of budgets and operational and functional definitions of ceremonial projects;

III – Technical and financial feasibility studies for the implementation of ceremonial projects and programs;

IV – Supervision and control of ceremonial activities;

V – Technical support and consulting in ceremonial matters;

VI – Studies, analyses, evaluations, inspections, opinions, expert examinations, and audits of ceremonial projects and programs;

VII – Teaching, research, experimentation, and dissemination of new instruments, standards, and procedures;

VIII – Any other activity that, by its nature, falls within the scope of their professions.

Article 3: The ceremonialist professional responsible for a plan, project, or program has the right to accompany its execution and implementation to ensure it is carried out according to the conditions, specifications, and technical details established.

Article 4: The workweek for the professionals covered by this law shall not exceed forty hours per week, with the possibility of compensating hours and reducing the workweek through collective bargaining agreements or labor agreements.

Article 5: This law shall enter into force on the date of its publication.

