The National Congress decrees:

CHAPTER I
OF THE PROFESSION OF ARCHAEOLOGIST

Article 1. This law regulates the practice of the profession of Archaeologist throughout the national territory.

Article 2º O. The practice of the profession of Archaeologist is exclusive:

I - to those graduated in Archaeology, from official or recognized schools by the Ministry of Education;

II - to those graduated in Archaeology from foreign schools recognized by the laws of the country of origin, whose titles have been validated in Brazil, in accordance with the relevant legislation;

III - to those with a post-graduate degree from schools or courses duly recognized by the Ministry of Education, with a concentration in Archaeology, and a master's thesis or doctoral dissertation on Archaeology, and at least 2 (two) consecutive years of proven scientific activity in the professional field of Archaeology;

IV - to those graduated in other higher-level courses who, at the time of the signing of this Law, have at least 5 (five) consecutive years or 10 (ten) years of intercalated scientific activity in the professional field of Archaeology.

V. Those who, as of the date of enactment of this Law, have completed specialized archaeology courses recognized by the Ministry of Education, with at least 3 (three) consecutive years of scientific activities specific to the professional field of Archaeology, duly proven.

§ 1. The proof referred to in items IV and V shall be verified by the contracting authority or company.

§ 2º O. The proof period referred to in the previous paragraph shall not be considered an impediment to the continuity of work for those in the process of fulfilling requirements.

Article 3. The duties of archaeologists include:

I. Planning, organizing, managing, directing, and supervising archaeological research activities;

II. Identifying, recording, prospecting, excavating, and conducting surveys of archaeological sites;

III. Performing analysis, classification, interpretation, and scientific information services of archaeological interest;

IV. Ensuring compliance with legislation governing archaeological activities in the country;

V. Coordinating, leading, supervising, and managing archaeological sectors in government institutions of direct and indirect public administration, as well as in private entities, as provided for in Article 9 of this Law;

VI. Providing consulting and advisory services in the field of Archaeology;

VII. Conducting appraisals to determine the scientific and cultural value of archaeological interests, as well as their authenticity;

VIII. Guiding, supervising, and implementing training, improvement, and specialization programs for individuals qualified in the field of Archaeology;

IX. Guiding the organization of seminars, colloquia, competitions, and exhibitions of national or international scope in the field of Archaeology, representing the institutions involved;

X. Preparing opinions related to matters of interest in the field of Archaeology;

XI. Coordinating, supervising, and leading projects and programs in the field of Archaeology.

Article 4. The hiring, appointment, or performance of technical positions, jobs, or functions shall be governed by the provisions of this Law.

BILL NO. 8,046-A, OF 2017
(From Mr. Ricardo Izar)
Amends Law No. 8,036, of May 11, 1990 (which provides for the Guarantee Fund for Time of Service and other provisions), to allow the movement of the linked account of the Guarantee Fund for Time of Service (FGTS) of a worker suffering from a chronic degenerative disease; amends Law No. 8,213, of July 24, 1991 (which provides for Social Security benefit plans and other provisions), to include chronic degenerative diseases among those that entitle to the non-applicability of waiting periods for the grant of sickness benefits and disability retirement; amends Law No. 8,899, of June 29, 1994 (which grants free passes to people with disabilities in the interstate public transportation system), to extend this benefit to people with chronic degenerative diseases; amends Law No. 7,713, of December 22, 1988 (which amends income tax legislation and other provisions), to extend to people with chronic degenerative diseases the benefit of exemption from income tax on their retirement or disability benefits; amends Law No. 8,989, of February 24, 1995 (which provides for the exemption from Industrialized Product Tax - IPI on the acquisition of automobiles for use in autonomous passenger transportation, as well as by people with physical disabilities, and other provisions), to include among the exempt people those with chronic degenerative diseases; and amends Law No. 8,383, of December 30, 1991 (which establishes the Fiscal Reference Unit, amends income tax legislation and other provisions), to extend the exemption from Interest on Operations with Foreign Currency - IOF to people with chronic degenerative diseases. It has the opinion of the Commission on Labor, Administration and Public Service, for approval with an amendment (report: DEP. CABO SABINO).

